Chapter 26 – Part 3
Curtis kept his body turned toward the bar so Mika would not have to watch the math happen on his face.
The pieces lined up whether he wanted them to or not.
Okay. So she was running from Shizuo and Celty.
That was confirmed. The text. The girl’s own mouth.
She has a scar on her neck.
Deliberate. Not an accident. Built on purpose so the story would look right.
She says she saw something she wasn’t supposed to see.
Same shape as Namie’s second half. A study. A thing in a jar. A boy who walked toward it.
Celty is chasing her.
Not a rumor. A request on his phone. Keep an eye out. Scar on her neck.
Yagiri is involved with human trafficking.
Shadows. Logistics. People with no paper trail. Labs that needed bodies and silence.
Celty’s missing her head.
The thought sat there like a live wire.
Mika’s face was reconstructed to be someone named Celty.
Curtis’s face went pale.
The bottles in front of him stopped being a shopping list. They became glass. Jars. A shelf in an uncle’s study. A reason a sister would pull a head out of a house and put it in a company. A reason a brother would steal it. A reason a missing classmate would sit down under a knife and get up wearing another woman’s face.
Yagiri has the head.
Not a guess anymore. A structure. Trafficking for research. A reconstructed girl as a substitute. A Black Rider hunting a scar because the scar was a map back to what had been taken from her.
His hand tightened on the edge of the bar.
Behind him, Mika’s voice came careful.
“…Curtis? You went pale. Did I— did I say something wrong?”
He did not turn all the way. He did not put the conclusion in the room with her. Not yet. Not while she was still learning how to answer to Mika and eat cake.
“No,” he said, and heard how thin it sounded. “Just putting the city together. You’re okay. Stay there.”
On the fifth floor, strawberry sugar still hung in the air.
In Curtis Nightstar’s head, the hunt finally had a center —
and it was a head that did not belong to the girl on his bed,
locked somewhere in the same family that had already tried to own her face.
Curtis stared at the row of bottles and did not see them.
This is bad. And getting complicated.
Head. Lab. Trafficking. A reconstructed classmate on his bed. A dullahan on his phone. A Yagiri sister who had wiped blood off his temple and asked him to watch for her brother. Anri still looking. Shizuo throwing things in the street. Two Gameboys that would not get built tonight.
Elsewhere, Justice snorted like the conclusion had been available at the airport.
Maybe you shouldn’t have left the Undead in L.A. then, idiot.
Curtis, who could not hear him, only breathed out through his nose and let the insult land anyway, as if some part of him had been waiting for it.
Behind him, Mika found the suite’s stereo.
She did not ask. She padded over, white cap still on, and touched the panel like a person testing whether the room was allowed to be a room. Soft music spilled out — nothing that belonged to a chase, nothing that belonged to a lab. Just sound, filling the high-roller quiet until the pale look on Curtis’s face had somewhere else to sit.
“…It’s too silent when you think that hard,” she said, not looking at him. “Feels like the door’s about to get kicked in.”
Curtis let the music take a layer off the math.
“Yeah. Good call.”
Justice, unheard, kept going.
Complicated is what happens when you run from the only four people who already know how you fight. Enjoy your solo album, bro.
On the fifth floor, Mika Harima turned the volume up one notch.
Curtis Nightstar kept the head to himself.
And the Undead stayed in another country, exactly as far away as a month-old leaving had put them —
which was, tonight, too far.
Mika turned from the stereo, music still low behind her.
“Are you the boy that was busking a few days back?”
Curtis blinked.
“Huh. You know about that?”
The question came out more startled than he meant. Long black braids sat in plain sight now — the same braids that had fallen out of a hood on a street when a crowd gathered around a guitar and a mask that did not quite hold.
Mika’s mouth tugged, almost shy, almost accusing.
“Hard to miss. Tall. Voice like that. Then the hood came down and everybody saw the hair.” She touched her own cap brim. “I was at the edge of it. Seiji wanted to keep moving. I wanted to stay. You were singing about staying undead and not calling it quits, and I thought… I don’t know. That it was weird a stranger could sound like he meant it.”
A short breath.
“Then I disappeared. Then I got this face. Then I crash into you and you hide me and buy cake and say my name like I’m allowed to have it.” She looked at him properly. “So yeah. I know about that. I just didn’t know the busking boy and the alley boy were the same person until the braids.”
Curtis huffed a quiet sound.
“Small city.”
“No,” Mika said. “Just a loud one. And you were loud that night. In a good way.”
The stereo kept playing.
On the fifth floor, the memorial songs from a street corner found the missing girl who had been standing at the edge of the crowd —
and Curtis Nightstar realized even his disguises had already been in her life before she ever ran into his arms.
Curtis’s mouth tugged.
“Hmm. Loud like a dove and a grenade.”
The smile that followed was soft — not the sidewalk smile, not the inventory smile. The one that belonged to a street corner, a guitar, and four people who were not in Japan to hear the line land again.
Mika tilted her head.
“…That’s from the song.” Not a question. She had been at the edge of the crowd. She had heard it. “You said it like it was a person, not a lyric.”
Curtis looked at the stereo, then at nothing.
“Yeah. It’s both.” He left the rest in the memorial box where it belonged. “Some things stay loud even after the street goes quiet.”
Mika watched the smile fade to something smaller and did not pry it open.
“Well,” she said, turning the volume a notch down so the suite could hold it, “you were loud that night. And you’re quiet now. I think I like this version better for a locked door. Save the grenade for when Shizuo finds the lobby.”
Curtis’s soft smile came back, just a little.
“Deal.”
On the fifth floor, a dove-and-grenade vow sat in the air between strawberry cake and a missing girl —
and for a moment the busking boy and the alley boy were allowed to be the same person without either of them having to explode.
Curtis tipped his chin toward the door, then the floor below.
“By the way. No one’s finding us.” Long black braids shifted as he said it without theater. “I didn’t use my real name downstairs, and I didn’t use Mika or Celty for my guest with me. That’s why I chose this place. As long as you got the money, they don’t ask questions.”
Mika’s shoulders loosened a fraction she had not given the chain.
“…So I’m not in the computer as me.” Relief first. Then the other thought. “And you’re not in it as you. Two fake people eating cake in a real suite.”
A short breath.
“That’s… actually smart. Seiji’s family would look for my name. The rider would look for hers. Shizuo would look for a fight, not a ledger.” She glanced at him. “What did you put me down as?”
Curtis’s mouth tugged.
“A friend. That’s all the desk needed. Friend doesn’t ping a lab and it doesn’t ping a motorcycle.”
Mika nodded once, filing it like a new rule for staying alive.
“Okay. Then I can sit here and be Mika out loud, and downstairs I can be nobody.” She looked at the stereo, the two beds, the bags from the kitchen. “Don’t go broke hiding me. If the money’s a storm story, I don’t want to be the reason it gets spent faster.”
Curtis shrugged, easy.
“One night in a place that doesn’t ask questions is cheaper than a street that does.”
On the fifth floor, two names that were not on the registry shared a room.
Downstairs, the ledger had a friend and a foreigner —
and nothing that would tell Namie, Celty, or Shizuo where the scar had gone.
Curtis waved a hand, dismissing the worry like it was a fly.
“Nah. Don’t worry about that.” Long black braids shifted as his mouth tipped. “Plus, if I run out, I’ll bill your family. You’re actually rich.”
Mika stared at him for a second.
Then she chuckled — a real one, startled out of her, the first sound the suite had gotten that did not belong to a chase.
“That’s not even my money.” She shook her head, white cap brim dipping. “That’s Seiji’s sister’s money. That’s lab money. That’s the worst tab in Ikebukuro and you’re joking about sending them an invoice for strawberry cake.”
A smaller laugh after it.
“…Don’t actually bill Yagiri. They’d send men instead of a receipt.” She glanced at him, still almost smiling. “But thank you. For making it sound stupid instead of heavy. I needed that more than the cake.”
Curtis’s grin flickered.
“Invoice says dango, two beds, one missing person, rush delivery. They can fight me in accounting.”
Mika snorted into her hand.
On the fifth floor, the joke held.
Outside, the family he had offered to bill was still hunting a brother and a scar.
Inside, Mika Harima laughed anyway —
and let a foreign student be ridiculous on purpose so she could remember what her own voice sounded like when it was not begging for help.
A new song came on.
Mika knew it. Or her body did. She was off the bed before the first chorus finished, white cap still on, pink tie swinging as she danced in the space between the two mattresses like the suite had briefly agreed to be a room instead of a hideout. Not a performance. Not a reconstruction. Just a missing girl moving because the music had given her permission.
Curtis kept his back turned at the bar.
He swirled the iced water in his glass until the cubes clicked, long black braids shifting against the blue-shadowed glass.
“I don’t need to have it all figured out right away.” The words went into the bottles, not the room. “That’s my problem.”
Head. Lab. Namie. Celty. Shizuo. Anri. Mikado. Masaomi. A car he would not buy. A brother he would not spend. A girl dancing behind him who had crashed into his arms and turned a Gameboy afternoon into a fifth-floor war.
He did not need the whole map tonight.
He needed the door locked, the names off the registry, and one night where Mika Harima got to be a person with a stereo.
The ice turned in the glass.
Behind him, Mika danced without asking if she was allowed.
Curtis Nightstar drank water in a fancy room and practiced not solving Ikebukuro in a single hour —
which, for him, was its own kind of discipline.
Curtis’s eyes snagged on one label and stayed there a second too long.
Suntory. Gold in the low light. The kind of bottle that would have made a quieter night worth remembering.
Curse your good manners.
That Suntory whisky looks good…
Bummer.
He swirled the iced water again like it might become something else if he was patient. It did not. Behind him Mika was still dancing, music filling the suite, the locked door holding, a missing girl moving like the city had not caught her yet.
Pouring now would make him a different kind of host.
The kind who opened a bar while someone else’s life was sitting on his bed waiting to see if he would disappear.
Curtis turned the bottle with one finger so the label faced away from him, a small, petty defeat.
“Water it is,” he muttered, and drank.
On the fifth floor, good manners won.
The Suntory stayed shut.
The shopping list got another line.
And Curtis Nightstar kept his head clear for a girl who needed him sober more than he needed a taste of something expensive.
Mika grabbed his arm.
Not hard. Not a plea from the alley. A pull from a girl who had decided the stereo was winning.
“If we are here,” she said, white cap tipping up at him, “you might as well follow your own saying. When in Rome.”
Curtis was surprised.
The iced water paused halfway to his mouth. Long black braids shifted as he looked down at her hand on his sleeve, mismatched eyes catching up a second late. People asked him for help. For hiding. For money. For names. They did not ask him to dance.
He had not been asked in quite some time.
“…You’re serious.”
“You’re standing at a bar drinking water in a fancy room while the music’s on.” Mika tugged once more, lighter. “You told me I don’t have to be a replacement. You don’t have to be a door guard for one song. When in Rome. Come on.”
The Suntory stayed shut.
The hunt stayed outside.
The good manners that had blocked the whisky did not have a clean argument against this.
Curtis set the glass down.
“One song,” he said, and let her pull him off the bar rail. “Then I go back to not having it all figured out.”
Mika’s mouth tugged.
“One song. Then you can brood at the bottles again.”
On the fifth floor, a missing girl and a missing-poster boy stepped into the space between two beds —
and Curtis Nightstar, who had survived guns and forests and a month-old leaving, discovered that being asked to dance still knew how to startle him.
They danced.
Not polished. Not a performance for anyone who might be watching through glass. Curtis moved like a boy who had learned rhythm on streets and in rooms with Justice, then put most of it away for a year of surviving. Mika moved like someone remembering she had a body that was allowed to want something other than to run. The stereo carried them through the space between the two beds, her hand still on his sleeve, his other hand unsure where to land until it settled light and decent at the air beside her shoulder.
In her mind, the comparison arrived and would not leave.
Why couldn’t Seiji be more like this?
Not taller. Not richer. Not louder.
Kinder.
The foreign boy who had been walking to a workshop with a smile, building Gameboys for the dead, and then stopped — all of it — because a girl crashed into his arms and said help me. Who hid her. Who paid for a room that did not ask her name. Who brought cake and dango and said Mika like it was allowed. Who stood at a bar drinking water so he would stay clear-headed for her, then let himself be pulled into a song anyway.
She had known Seiji longer.
She had changed her face for him.
And still this stranger, a handful of hours old in her life, was doing the part she had wanted without being asked to love a neck.
Mika looked at Curtis’s braids, at the mismatch of his eyes when they caught the hotel light, and kept the thought where he could not hear it.
He just met me. And he still helps me.
Seiji knew me. And he helped the version of me that looked like someone else.
The song kept going.
Curtis did not ask what was on her face. He only danced the one song he had promised, careful not to turn it into a claim.
On the fifth floor, Mika Harima danced with the kind of boy she had wanted Seiji Yagiri to be —
and hated, a little, how easy the wanting was when the kindness did not require surgery to keep.
The song ended.
Curtis let go first, already turning toward the bar, toward the bottles and the next move — Mikado, Masaomi, Anri as a bridge, the head, the hunt, the morning.
He did not make it.
Mika grabbed his hand.
Not the sleeve this time. His fingers. Warm. A little sticky from sugar. The white cap tipped up so he had to look at her instead of the Suntory.
“Wait.” A short breath. “Before you go stand over there and put the whole city back together in your head.”
She swallowed.
“When morning comes… am I still Mika to you? Or do I go back to being a problem you hid for one night?” Her grip tightened once, not trapping, just asking. “And why are you like this with someone you just met? People don’t stop their whole day for a girl who crashes into them. Seiji didn’t. So I need to know if this is who you are, or if I’m going to wake up and find out I made you into another story I wanted.”
The suite held the question.
Curtis stood between the dance floor and the bar with a missing girl’s hand in his, the next move waiting in the glass bottles —
and Mika Harima, who had already been reconstructed once, asked him not to reconstruct her kindness before she knew whether it would survive daylight.
Curtis did not pull his hand away.
“You’re not a problem, Mika. And I don’t see you that way at all.” Long black braids shifted as mismatched eyes held hers. “You will be Mika to me, if that’s okay with you.”
A short breath.
“As for why I stopped and helped…” He shrugged, small, almost embarrassed by how simple it was. “’Cause it’s the right thing to do. You have had all this time to run from yourself, but you didn’t.”
He tipped his head.
“And what do you perceive me as? I’m curious.”
Mika’s fingers flexed in his.
The honest answer sat on her tongue and scared her more than Shizuo.
“…I perceive you as the kind of person I kept waiting for someone else to be.” It came out careful, then flatter when she refused to dress it up. “You stopped. You hid me. You bought food. You said my name. You danced because I asked. You didn’t make me earn it with a face.”
A thinner beat.
“I don’t mean that as a trap. I don’t know you well enough to put a word on you. Not boyfriend. Not savior. Not Seiji with better manners.” She looked at their hands, then up. “I perceive you as someone who stays in the room. That’s rarer than money in this city. That’s what I see. If that’s too much, you can go back to the bar and I can pretend I said a nice stranger instead.”
Curtis let the answer sit.
On the fifth floor, Mika Harima had not called him a replacement.
She had called him the thing she had been starving for —
and she had given him an exit in the same breath, because she had already learned what happened when you held a boy too tight and called it love.
Curtis’s ears went a little warm.
“That’s actually very sweet.” Long black braids shifted as he looked at their hands instead of her face, then forced himself back up. “I— I don’t know what to say, Mika.”
The stammer sat badly on a boy who had talked Namie down from a gun and Shizuo’s chase into a locked suite. This was different. This was not strategy. This was a missing girl calling him someone who stays in the room.
Mika blinked, then a small, startled smile showed up like it had been waiting for him to trip.
“…You’re blushing.” Not cruel. Wondering. “The guy who hid me in fog and joked about billing Yagiri for cake doesn’t know what to say because I called him kind.”
She did not let go of his hand.
“You don’t have to say anything back. I wasn’t asking you to be that person on purpose. I was telling you what I already see.” A short breath. “You can go to the bar now. Figure out the city. I’ll stay Mika. You stay the boy who doesn’t know what to say. That’s… fine. That’s better than a speech.”
Curtis huffed a quiet, flustered sound.
“Yeah. Okay.” He squeezed her fingers once, careful, then nodded toward the bottles without moving yet. “Still not a problem. Still Mika. I just— wasn’t ready for sweet.”
On the fifth floor, the hunt stayed outside.
Inside, Curtis Nightstar ran out of words for the first time all day —
and Mika Harima looked at him like the stammer was proof she had not imagined the kindness after all.
Curtis found a few more words and used them carefully.
“Mika. When we get through this, let’s get another room down the line.” Long black braids shifted as he made sure the next part landed clean. “Nothing intimate in that sense. But next time let’s just hang out. Instead of being in a situation like this.”
A short, almost shy beat.
“You’re really fun to hang out with.”
Mika stared at him.
The suite, the chase, the reconstruction, the stammer — and then a boy offering her a future that was just a room and sweets and no one hunting a scar.
“…A room for hanging out,” she repeated, like she had to check the translation. “Not a hideout. Not a lab. Not a place I have to be Celty.”
Her mouth tugged.
“You’re booking a next time like I’m not a disaster.” A thinner breath, then a nod. “Okay. When we get through this. Another room. Cake. Dango. Music. You can window-shop the bar and I can dance and you don’t have to hide me from a rider to justify the night.”
She squeezed his hand once.
“Nothing intimate. I heard you. I needed to hear you. People skip that part and then the room turns into a debt.” A smaller smile. “You’re fun too. When you’re not going pale at the bottles. I’d hang out with that version on purpose.”
Curtis’s ears stayed a little warm.
“Then that’s the plan. Survive first. Hang out after.”
On the fifth floor, the high-roller suite stopped being only a cage with good linens.
It became a draft of a next time —
two beds, no hunt, a girl named Mika, and a boy who had finally said she was fun instead of only in danger.
Mika smiled at the plan and kept the next sentence where he could not hear it.
But what if I want it to be intimate…
The thought arrived uninvited, sticky as sugar, and she hated how fast it had shown up after a boy said nothing intimate in that sense like he was protecting her. Maybe he was. Maybe he was protecting a list of other names she did not own. Maybe he just knew a hunted girl on a fifth floor was the worst place in Ikebukuro to turn kindness into a bed.
She looked at their hands, then let his go before the wanting could make her grip dishonest.
He said hang out. He said I’m fun. He said Mika.
That should be enough.
Seiji never offered a next time that wasn’t about the face.
The private question still sat there.
What if I want the thing he just took off the table?
Mika turned toward the stereo so Curtis would see her back instead of the heat in her expression.
“Survive first,” she said out loud, agreeing with the safe version. “Hang out after.”
On the fifth floor, she accepted the boundary with her mouth.
In her chest, she filed a different next time —
one she was not going to ask for tonight,
not from a boy who had only just learned how to say her name,
and not in a room that still had a chain on the door.
